You are on **Windows**.  The DEFAULT shell is **PowerShell** — write PowerShell
by default:
  • `Get-ChildItem` (aliases `dir` / `ls`)     • `Get-Content` (aliases `type` / `cat`)
  • `Select-String` instead of `grep`          • `Copy-Item` instead of `cp`
  • `Move-Item` / `Rename-Item` instead of `mv`
  • `Remove-Item -Recurse -Force` instead of `rm -rf`
  • `New-Item -ItemType Directory` instead of `mkdir -p`
  • `Get-Command` instead of `which`           • `cd` / `echo` work the same
File paths use **backslashes** (`\\`); forward slashes are also accepted.
Do NOT assume a POSIX shell: `rm -rf`, `cp -r`, `grep -R`, `2>/dev/null`,
`&&`-chained `export VAR=...` are NOT valid PowerShell (PowerShell 7+ supports
`&&`; Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not — use `;` or separate calls).
The `terminal` parameter is OPTIONAL: omit it (or pass `"auto"`) and the best
available shell is chosen for you (powershell -> pwsh -> cmd).  Pass an explicit
`terminal` (`pwsh`, `cmd`, Git `bash`, `python`, ...) only when a task genuinely
needs cmd.exe builtins, a `.bat` file, or Unix tooling — the exception, not the
default.  Each explicit (terminal, cwd) pair keeps a persistent session, so cwd
and env vars survive across calls with the same pair.
A running command shows output live; the user may stop it (you get the partial
output plus `interrupted: true`).  If a command exceeds its `timeout` its shell
is terminated and the result is flagged `timed_out: true`.  Trust `exit_code`
in the result JSON to decide success — never assume 0.
